# Single‐Atom Catalysts Originated from Metal–Organic Frameworks for Sulfate Radical‐Based Advanced Oxidation Processes: Critical Insights into Mechanisms

## abstract
The widely discussed single‐atom catalysts (SACs) are regarded as a kind of attractive material for sulfate radical‐based advanced oxidation processes (SR‐AOPs) owing to their maximum atomic utilization efficiency and outstanding stability. Currently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have appeared as prospective precursors for building SACs due to extensive chelating sites, functional adjustability, and structural tunability. However, there are few critical and systematic reviews about the application of MOF‐derived SACs in SR‐AOPs, especially in‐depth analysis of the mechanisms. Therefore, this review seeks to offer a thorough summary of the development of MOF‐derived SACs in SR‐AOPs. First, the unique advantages of MOFs as derivative precursors for SACs are discussed thoroughly. Afterward, the current synthesis strategies are elaborated categorically to unveil the formation process of single atoms and their coordination environments. Notably, the roles of different reaction sites including the generation of reactive species and mediating electron transfer are further analyzed to explain mechanisms comprehensively. Thereafter, the characterization techniques and theoretical calculations for mechanisms studies are also highlighted. Eventually, critical insights into present challenges and future developments are proposed, which are expected to enhance the catalytic efficiency of MOF‐derived SACs in SR‐AOPs.

The widely discussed single-atom catalysts (SACs) are regarded as a kind of attractive material for sulfate radical-based advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs) owing to their maximum atomic utilization efficiency and outstanding stability. Currently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have appeared as prospective precursors for building SACs due to extensive chelating sites, functional adjustability, and structural tunability. However, there are few critical and systematic reviews about the application of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs, especially in-depth analysis of the mechanisms. Therefore, this review seeks to offer a thorough summary of the development of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs. First, the unique advantages of MOFs as derivative precursors for SACs are discussed thoroughly. Afterward, the current synthesis strategies are elaborated categorically to unveil the formation process of single atoms and their coordination environments. Notably, the roles of different reaction sites including the generation of reactive species and mediating electron transfer are further analyzed to explain mechanisms comprehensively. Thereafter, the characterization techniques and theoretical calculations for mechanisms studies are also highlighted. Eventually, critical insights into present challenges and future developments are proposed, which are expected to enhance the catalytic efficiency of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs.
## 1. Introduction
With rapid industrialization, pesticides, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, pharmaceuticals, personal care products, and other persistent organic pollutants have drawn wide attention due to their high toxicity and severe threat to the environment.[1] It is crucial to control the propagation of these organic pollutants in water due to their bio-enrichment and stability.[2] However, conventional wastewater treatment technologies are not able to reach desirable performance.[3] Thus, it is critical to investigate more effective and ecologically acceptable treatment options to get rid of persistent organic pollutants.[4]
Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) have shown great simplicity, effectiveness, and stability since they were first introduced in 1987,[5] as they are capable of producing numerous extremely reactive oxygen species (ROS) including singlet oxygen $({}^{1}\mathsf{O}_{2})$ , hydroxyl radicals (•OH), and sulfate radicals $(\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{\bullet-})$ .[6] In particular, sulfate radical-based advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs) are considered an alternative to traditional •OH-based AOPs due to their unique advantages:[7] I) the half-life of $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ $(30-40~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ is longer than that of •OH $(<1~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ ;[8] II) $\mathrm{SO_{4}^{\bullet-}}$ exhibits a higher oxidation potential $(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\bullet-}$ , 2.6–3.1 V, •OH, 1.8–2.7 V) and reaction rate with organic substances over a broad $\mathtt{p H}$ range $(k_{\mathrm{SO4bullet-}}$ $\:\:=\:10^{5}–10^{9}\:\mathrm{\:M^{-1}\:\:s^{-1}}\:$ ; $k_{\bullet\mathrm{OH}}=10^{6}{-}10^{11}\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{s}^{-1});\mathbf{\u{s}}^{[9]}$ and III) $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ has a higher selectivity to organic compounds with particular electron-donating groups (alkoxy, hydroxyl, and amino, etc.) which attributed to only one electron transfer pathway.[8c,10] Up to now, $\mathrm{SO_{4}^{\bullet-}}$ catalysts have been employed to activate peroxymonosulfate (PMS, $\mathrm{HSO}_{5}^{-}$ ) or peroxydisulfate (PDS, $\mathsf{S}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}{}^{2-})$ to produce $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ .[11] In this sense, a popular area of research about SR-AOPs has been searching for extremely effective and stable catalysts.[3b] Recently, common catalysts mainly include homogenous catalysts (e.g., $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+})^{[12]}$ and heterogenous catalysts (metal nanoparticles).[13]
However, homogenous catalysts are not easily collected and recovered as soluble catalysts after use.[3b] In addition, heterogenous catalysts show lower atomic efficiency than homogenous catalysts due to a significant portion of metals being embedded within the particles as opposed to being exposed to the reaction fluid.[14] Fortunately, single-atom catalysts (SACs) have developed in the past few years which retain the benefits of heterogenous and homogenous catalysts.[15] It also could be reused and reach $100\%$ maximum atom utilization efficiency.[2b,16] SACs are prepared by anchoring metal catalysts on the supports via strong ligand binding in an atomically dispersed way. To eliminate metal–metal interactions and completely expose each atom to the reaction media, SACs are constructed by attaching metal catalysts to the supports by strong ligand binding in an atomically distributed way.[17] Carbon-based materials are frequently employed as precursors to form SACs due to abundant anchoring sites and flexible surface properties.[18] Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), as 3D carbon materials, are crystalline porous materials composed of metal sites and organic ligands that are arranged in a periodic network structure.[9b,19] Owing to an excessive number of ordered frameworks and unsaturated metal active sites,[20] MOFs and their composites can be transformed into MOFderived SACs through simple pyrolysis or post-modification.[21] During this interesting transformation, the nitrogen in the precursor MOFs can covalently interact with the metal,[22] which not only largely avoids aggregation between metals but also improves the stability and loadings of SACs.[23]
Currently, plenty of studies have investigated the synthesis methods, characterization, and applications of SACs.[1b,24] To the best of our knowledge, current advances have been made lately in the synthesis, in situ characterization, and photocatalytic and electrochemical applications of SACs.[1b,24a,25] Wu and $\mathrm{Kim}^{[3\mathrm{b}]}$ provided critical views on the application of SR-AOPs, especially regarding the mechanisms of how persulfate (PS) reacts with SACs different from corresponding nanoparticle counterparts. Nevertheless, SACs associated with MOFs have not received sufficient attention. In this regard, Tang et al.[26] and Qu et al.[24c] have overviewed recent advances in MOF-based materials for environmental remediation involving MOF-based SACs. However, the utilization of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs is still in the elementary stage.[24c,27] Although both Xia et al.[24b] and Li and Liu[28] summarized the application of MOF-derived SACs in diverse AOPs, the in-depth study of SR-AOPs is not comprehensive enough which facilitates the development of catalysts with higher performance. For bridging this gap, a timely review of the recent developments of MOF-derived SACs in the realm of SR-AOPs is highly imperative, which will provide a reference for future research directions. Herein, this review is first dedicated to giving a thorough summary of the unique benefits of MOFs as excellent derived precursors. Meanwhile, approaches for the fabrication of MOF-derived SACs are systematically outlined to clarify their formation process as well as coordination environment. Second, the mechanisms of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs are in-depth analyzed, including the advanced techniques for the identification of ROS and their corresponding generation pathways. Finally, we concisely conclude our review and propose existing challenges and related perspectives from material design to practical application. This review will become a bright star in this area and expand knowledge of MOF-derived SACs applied to SR-AOPs, leading to rapid development in this emerging direction.
## 2. Synthesis of MOF-Derived SACs
Currently, the challenge and the crucial research direction to apply SACs in catalysis is the fabrication of stable single atoms. The emergence of MOFs can enhance the stability and loading capacity of single atoms by modifying or controlling synthetic conditions. Up to now, diverse strategies have been devised to obtain SACs by derivatizing MOFs, among which pyrolysis is regarded as one of the synthesizing-simple methods.[29] In some cases, post-modification may be required after pyrolysis.[21] Herein, this section begins with a thorough summary of the advantages of MOFs as superior precursors. Afterward, two common fabrication strategies, including direct pyrolysis and post-modification, are comprehensively presented (Scheme 1). In particular, more attention is paid to how the MOFs are derived into SACs.
## 2.1. Prospective Precursors of SACs
Compared to conventional catalysts, SACs are substances with atomic metal species stabilized on suitable supports, which could provide maximum atomic efficiency and reusability.[30] However, dispersed metal atoms tend to aggregate during construction owing to their higher surface energies than metal nanoparticles and clusters.[31] Fortunately, it has been experimentally demonstrated that suitable supports can make single atoms retain high dispersion stability.[24] Currently, suitable supporting substrates mainly include carbon-based materials (e.g., MOFs,[32] covalent organic frameworks,[33] and biochar[22]), semiconductors (e.g., $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}^{[34]}$ and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}^{[35]}$ ), and other materials (e.g., metal oxides[36] and nanoparticles[37]). MOFs are one of the best precursors for preparing SACs due to optimal crystal framework, luxurious metal sites, high chemical tunability, and substantial specific surface area.[21,38] However, the framework of some MOFs will sometimes collapse in ultra-high temperature environments and single MOFs tend to show poor conductivity with a low degree of graphitization, while MOF derivatives can solve the above defects and expand the application fields.[39] Besides, MOF derivatives can prevent the aggregation of single atoms and maintain stability simultaneously.[24] In the following section, the dramatic functions of MOFs as one of the prospective precursors of SACs are summarized systematically (Scheme 2).
First of all, MOF-derived SACs succeed in the substantial specific surface area and porosity of the precursor MOFs. Therefore, it exhibits considerable potential in SR-AOPs by contributing adequate exposure of the active sites and efficient mass transfer. Besides, it is worth mentioning that most conventional SACs exhibit difficulty in controlling metal loading.[40] It is required to construct general and creative strategies to synthesize SACs with relatively high loading capacity. In this aspect, MOFs are also satisfactory precursors of SACs because they can load more single atoms by providing numerous available chelating sites.
In addition to increasing metal loading, reducing metal aggregation should not be ignored. MOFs have a unique structure with alternating inorganic sites and organic linkers,[41] which provide better stability for SACs. The simple pyrolysis and reduction would make the N of the parent MOFs form stable M–N coordination groups with single metal atoms, which largely keeps single atoms from aggregation as well as enhances the stability of the SACs.[42]
In general, MOF-derived SACs do not require additional templates and can conveniently obtain highly dispersed single atoms only by pyrolysis and post-modifications.[43] At the same time, MOFs can be adjusted simply by altering their structural components or modifying synthesis conditions. For instance, the spin states of single atoms will be altered by introducing inorganic elements (e.g., N,[44] P,[45] and $\mathsf{S}^{[13]}$ ) or modifying organic ligands.
It tends to lead the active ingredients in SACs exhibiting high atomic selectivity and reducing activity as well as facilitating proton transfer to boost a catalytic cycle.[46]
## 2.2. Direct Pyrolysis Synthesis
Direct pyrolysis synthesis is the most widely used approach for the fabrication of MOF-derived SACs and refers to direct pyrolysis of MOFs-loaded target metals under reducing or inert gas conditions. As MOFs are quite abundant in their coordination sites, target metal atoms were typically anchored at different coordination sites to achieve uniform dispersion and form the target SACs.[47] Therefore, this section focuses on the different positions of the MOFs where target metal species are introduced through pyrolysis, including metal nodes, organic linkers, and the cavities of MOFs.
Due to the uniformly dispersed metal sites and highly adjustable, the formation of bimetallic MOFs by introducing metal atoms into MOFs would be possible.[48] Among these MOFs, zeolite imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are ideal pioneers for the fabrication of SACs since their organic ligands contain a high content of N dopants and the Zn atom has a low boiling point.[49] In addition, its highly fragmented structure can make the introduced metal atoms easily incorporated into ZIFs and dispersed at a certain distance.[29] Yin and co-workers[50] synthesized Co single atoms/nitrogen-doped porous carbon (Co SAs-N/C) by direct pyrolysis of $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetallic MOF precursors (Figure 1a). A certain percentage of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ sites was replaced by the addition of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ , which served as a “fence” to prevent Co aggregation. Besides, Zn was evaporated above $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while Co SAs was synthesized in situ at the carbon sites through strong covalent bonds. It confirmed that the introduction of metals possessed a positive effect on the formation of SACs. Similarly, Se SACs on MOF-derived NC frameworks $(\mathrm{Se}\operatorname{SAs}@\mathrm{NC})$ were formed by Hu et al.[51] with the formed Se species were trapped and anchored on the NC framework surface. Particularly, nonmetallic doping can simply be achieved in the construction of MOF precursors containing the target metals due to the space-size effect. Yang et al.[52] successfully obtained N, P-coordinated single-atom catalysts (FeSAs/PNC) by using triphenylphosphine $\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\right)$ , which could be effectively encapsulated in cages and combined with Fe atoms. According to the Fe K-edge X-ray near-edge absorption (XANES) technique and wavelet transform analysis, the P element was uniformly distributed in Fe-SAs/PNC and the coordination structure of the Fe-SAs/PNC was Fe- $\mathbf{\cdotP}_{1}\mathbf{N}_{3}$ . Apart from the metal atoms, the pyrolysis temperature also could affect the generation of SACs by influencing the coordination environment. Liang et al.[53] synthesized a series of Co catalysts with different nitrogen coordination numbers (denoted as $\mathbf{Co-N}_{X})$ under different pyrolysis temperatures. Specifically, the pyrolysis temperatures of 1073, 1173, and 1273 K were corresponding to ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{3}$ , and ${\mathrm{Co}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{N}}_{2}$ , respectively (Figure 1b). The pre-edge centroid shifts slightly to lower energy on transitioning from ${\mathsf{C o}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ to Co$\Nu_{2}$ according to X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) and XANES. This phenomenon suggested higher pyrolysis temperatures lead to more reduction of Co atoms and breaking of $\mathrm{Co-N}$ coordination bonds. In addition to ZIFs, Prussian blue analogues (PBAs),[3,42] Dabco metal-organic framework (DMOF),[47,54] and Materials of Institute Lavoisier frameworks $({\mathrm{MILS}})^{[{55}]}$ are also excellent MOFs for the preparation of SACs.
Although SACs can be successfully obtained by using dispersed mixed metal precursors, some noble metals are rarely used as metal nodes in the preparation of MOFs.[56] Fortunately, organic ligands can act as “paws” in MOFs and exhibit significant advantages in coordination interactions with noble metal atoms. In general, organic linkers usually process atoms with one or more electronically lone pairs that bond with the desired metals, such as amino,[57] bipyridine,[58] and porphyrin groups,[59] which can operate as the coordination cages for metal species.[60] Jiao et al.[59] synthesized 3D network of pocket-channel frameworks with Fe-TCPP $(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}{\cdot}\mathrm{PCN})$ material by carbonizing a 1D mesoporous channel porphyrin-like MOF (PCN-222). In the synthesized materials, the phase change was favored by the mixed ligands of Fe-TCPP and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TCPP $\mathrm{(TCPP=}$ tetrakis (4- carboxyphenyl) porphyrin). Their 3D skeleton not only exhibited extremely high stability but effectively suppressed the molecular stacking, which was caused by the strong coordination interactions between the porphyrin and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . Furthermore, different M-N-C $\mathrm{TM}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, Ni, and Cu) with the PCN-222 were obtained using the mixed ligand strategy (Figure 1c).[61] Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) confirmed that the M-N-C all had rod-like shapes and possessed similar surface area and pore size distribution. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) also supported the existence of corresponding $\mathrm{\Delta\times-N}$ bonding. It should be emphasized that X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) illustrated that the four N atoms coordinated all of the singleatom M, creating a stable metal coordination environment. Likewise, He and colleagues[23a] fabricated a series of metal singleatom catalysts using PCN-222 as a precursor. In this design, a series of DPCN222(Co)-X (X represents the ratio of adding water) catalysts was prepared by mixing chemical compounds, and the comparative samples of different metal SACs were simply fabricated through varying the target metal species (Cu and Mn). At last, due to the well-defined cavity structure of the MOFs, space constraints have also been used to synthesize SACs by ensnaring metal molecules inside the MOF cavity.[62] For example, single-atom iron catalysts anchored on N-doped porous carbon (Fe-NC) were fabricated by pyrolyzing cage-encapsulated ZIF-8 where ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ molecule was trapped in towing to size space effects (Figure 1d).[63] After pyrolysis under the Ar atmosphere, ZIF-8 was turned into nitrogen-doped porous carbon due to the low boiling point of Zn. Simultaneously, ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ within the cage was reduced by carbonization, leading to the formation of isolated individual iron atoms anchored to the nitrogen species. Density functional theory (DFT) calculation and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) analysis confirmed the dispersed states of the Fe atoms and revealed that the coordination number of the single Fe atoms was 4 which had the most negative formation energy (Figure 1e). Noteworthy, this approach is not only suitable for the synthesis of individual $\mathrm{SACS^{[64]}}$ but also for dual-atom-site catalysts. Yang and coworkers[65] used two metal precursors and successfully prepared isolated diatomic Fe─Co (denoted $\operatorname{FeCo}\ @\operatorname{NC})$ anchored on Ndoped porous carbon substrates (Figure 1f). Additionally, they discovered that the aggregation of FeCo clustered into nanoparticles when the number of metal precursors was significantly increased. Besides, EXAFS analysis indicated that the first shell Fe–N coordination number was 3, while the Fe–Co coordination number was one. The above results led to the proposal of a unique diatomic configuration of the $\mathrm{FeCoN}_{6}$ identified in FeCo@NC.[65]
## 2.3. Post-Modification
Direct pyrolysis synthesis can successfully prepare SACs. However, in some cases, active metal atoms will be more accessible to aggregate together to form clusters when the quantity of target metal atoms exceeds the unformed chelation sites.[21] Therefore, strategies for post-modification have been proposed to fabricate MOF-derived SACs. Such strategies mainly include acidwashing strategy, top-down bulk metal preparation, and other post-modification methods.
## 2.3.1. Acid-Washing Strategy
Generally, the acid-washing strategy is the most commonly applied post-modification method which can remove metal clusters/particles to guarantee that the dispersed metal atoms are anchored to the MOF-derived SACs. Li et al.[42] used $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ washing to remove Fe–Co clusters from derivatives of Fe-Co PBA to synthesize the single-Co-atom catalysts (FeCo-NC) materials (Figure 2a). Field emission scanning electron microscope and Xray diffraction confirmed that FeCo-NC maintained a uniform morphology, inheriting well of Fe-Co PBA after acid-washing. In addition to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , HF and diluted HCl can also be used as washing agents. In the synthesis of an overhang-eave structure decorated with isolated iron atom sites (Fe/OES),[62a] both acids were applied to remove the metal nanoparticles generated after calcining and the shell (Figure 2b). Annular bright-field scanning transmission electron microscopy images of Fe/OES validated its suspended-eave morphology with abundant wellshaped edges. Meanwhile, high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) analysis confirmed the presence of isolated monatomic Fe sites in the structure.
However, the performance of SACs cannot reach the optimum after acid-washing in some cases because of the lack of a metal atomic level planar structure coordinated by four nitrogen atoms.[66] Fortunately, thermal activation can be employed to solve this problem by properly repairing the coordinated numbers of single atoms.[67] Yang and co-workers[68] synthesized the poly-dispersed Mn sites with unsaturated two nitrogen-coordinated centers $(\mathrm{Mn}\ \mathrm{SAs}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ) from $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}$ - 8, then obtained the four-nitrogen-coordinated Mn (Mn SAs$\mathrm{N}_{4}$ ) sites catalyst by the thermal activation under $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ atmosphere. The coordination center of the Mn single atom and symmetry of the oxidation state were significantly improved in Mn SAs- $\mathrm{N}_{4}$ compared with Mn $\mathsf{S A s}{\mathrm{-N}}_{2}$ , thus ensuring stability.
## 2.3.2. Top-Down Bulk Metal Preparation
The above methods aim to prevent collisions of the target metal species by removing these metal clusters/particles. Interestingly, recent studies have confirmed that metal clusters/particles could be used to synthesize SACs, which is called the “top-down” approach. In this approach, the metal–metal bonds in the metal nanoparticle precursors are disassembled by thermal treatment to form MOF-derived SACs.[69] Through heating under an inert atmosphere, Wei and co-workers[69] used nitrogen-doped carbon (NC) derived from ZIF-8 as the anchoring substrate to convert the precious metal nanoparticles into thermally stable and highly reactive single atoms (Pd, Pt, Au). HAADF-STEM images indicated that the size of the crystalline Pd-NPs first increased and then gradually disappeared due to changes in the crystal status during thermal treatment. In addition to metal nanoparticles, metal oxides are also able to diverse into isolated single atoms. In preparation for the isolated copper single-atom site catalysts $\mathrm{(Cu~ISAs/NC)}$ ,[69] $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{O}$ on the surface first evaporated to form volatile species under a flowing $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. The volatiles were then captured and reduced by NC derived from ZIF-8 to form the Cu ISAS/NC catalyst (Figure 3a). Due to the volatility of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{O}$ , the corrosiveness of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ generated by $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ was avoided. Additionally, it should be noted that “top-down” approaches are not only applicable to preparing the individual metal single atoms but also the bimetallic single atoms. Jiao and coworkers[69] synthesized Fe-Ni-N-C via a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -assisted atomization strategy (Figure 3b). In this case, the iron-doped $z\mathrm{nO}$ (Fe-ZnO) and nickel-doped ZnO (Ni-ZnO) NPs were assembled onto ZIF8, and the doped Fe and Ni atoms in $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO}$ were then trapped during pyrolysis to form Fe-Ni-N-C. The separated dispersion of Fe and Ni atoms in pairs was depicted by HAADF-STEM, consequently named single-atom pairs. Long-range electronic interaction served as the fundamental driving factor for the formation of Fe-Ni pairs, as confirmed by the L-edge near-edge X-ray absorption fine structure spectra and XPS data.
precursors followed by secondary pyrolysis, which improves the electron transfer and accessibility to single-metal sites.[21,72] Li et al.[70b] found an effective two-step pyrolysis and absorption procedure to engineer dual sites (i.e., Ni-Fe, Fe-Co, and Ni-Co) (Figure 3d). The ZIF-NC was prepared by carbonizing ZIF-8 nanocrystals under Ar atmosphere as the first pyrolysis, followed by the adsorptive capture of metal ions $(\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ) with nitrogen dopants and micropores. Subsequent pyrolysis (called secondary pyrolysis) under the Ar atmosphere promoted the bonding between Fe, Ni, and N embedded into the carbon planes. EXAFS and electron energy-loss spectroscopy demonstrated that the Fe and Ni SAs were embedded in the (Fe-Ni) $\mathrm{N}_{6}$ with pyridine N as the anchor location point (Figure 3c). Importantly, the singlemetal ions could be stabilized by pyridine $\mathsf{N}$ to form thermodynamically stable $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ active sites.
To sum up, MOFs are demonstrated to be suitable precursors due to their porous structure and abundant active sites. Moreover, pyrolysis is the most commonly used and straightforward approach to convert MOF precursors containing target metal species into single-metal atoms. In some cases, the postmodification approaches are used to ensure the uniformity of single atoms. Although MOF-derived single-atom synthesis methods become more sophisticated, some challenges are still to be resolved. I) It is crucial to vigorously develop additional types of MOF precursors such as MILs and University of Oslo (UiO) to expand the catalytic mechanisms of MOF-derived SACs. II) More research is still needed to determine how to increase the metal loading for MOF-derived SACs without aggregation. III) Focus on the role of the coordination environment. Up to now, the rational design and precise regulation of the coordination environments of SACs are indispensable conditions for their superior performance.[73] First of all, suitable coordination environments influence the catalytic reaction energy barrier and reaction pathway profoundly, thus significantly improving the catalytic performance.[74] In addition, coordination environments can also enhance the reaction selectivity by rational tailoring.[75] Second, the coordination environments of isolated single atoms can be further improved by rational design, thus stabilizing metal atoms and avoiding metal agglomeration.[76] Therefore, it is essential to investigate the coordination environments of SACs which greatly enriches the understanding of the catalytic activity and selectivity.[77]
## 2.3.3. Other Post-Modification Methods
Despite the methods described above, there are some other methods that have also been reported.[70] Zhang and co-workers[70a] proposed a facile post-synthetic metal substitution (PSMS) strategy. This method developed the single-atom Ni catalyst with a low coordination number on a predesigned NC support under regulated conditions. The single $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms could be easily removed due to the fragile $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ interactions in acidic media and the low boiling point of $Z\mathrm{n}$ . The remaining numerous $Z\mathrm{n}$ vacancies with $3\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms surrounded could be refiled by Ni atoms spontaneously. Notably, the PSMS strategy had promising universality for the preparation of SACs, as identified by the successful synthesis of Fe- ${\bf N}_{3}$ -C and Co- ${\bf N}_{3}$ -C.[70a] Moreover, all the above reports used primary pyrolysis, but secondary pyrolysis was also used in some recent studies.[70b,71] This strategy generally starts with the utilization of MOF-derived porous carbon to adsorb metal
## 3. Reaction Mechanisms of MOF-Derived SACs in SR-AOPs
Currently, MOF-derived SACs achieve the efficient degradation of pollutants and their catalytic mechanisms have received particular attention.[62,78] Table 1 summarizes various MOF-derived SACs applied in SR-AOPs. Specifically, the catalysts convert pollutants into small molecules (such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ) or less toxic substances mainly by activating PMS/PDS to generate ROS (e.g., •OH and $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ ) or enhancing the electron transfer process (ETP) on active sites (Scheme 3).[43,79] In addition, adjacent sites are also crucial in reducing the distance between the pollutants and the ROS.[64,80] Thus, this section will discuss the mechanism in detail according to the different sites. Moreover, some critical insights on ROS and ETP will be proposed.
## 3.1. Role of Active Sites
## 3.1.1. Generation of ROS
Radical: Current studies confirmed that radicals especially $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ and •OH are the main products of PS activation by MOFderived SACs.[90] Similar to the conventional interaction between dissolved metal ions with PS, an electron is transferred from the $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{x}$ of MOF-derived SACs to the coordinated O atom of the PS molecule due to the higher electronegativity of O than metals. Such single electron transfer leads to the lengthening and breaking of the O─O bond, which leads to producing $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ or •OH. The following are reaction equations for the production of free radicals (Equations (1) and (2)). The final step is the removal of intermediate products from the active sites and their regeneration after the activation of the PS absorbed on the surface, thus constituting a cycle of degradation.[3b]
$$
\mathbf{M}^{n+}\mathbf{+HSO}_{5}^{-}\rightarrow\mathbf{M}^{(n+1)+}\mathbf{+SO}_{4}^{-}\mathbf{+OH}
$$
$$
\mathrm{M}^{n+}{+}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}^{2-}\to\mathrm{M}^{(n+1)+}{+}\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}{+}\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}
$$
Yang et al.[64] developed $\operatorname{Ni-SAs@CN}$ through a micropore confinement strategy and first obtained a high sulfamethoxazole (SMX) removal efficiency in the Ni-SAs $@$ CN/PMS system ( $100\%$ within $20\mathrm{min}$ ), which was ascribed to the contribution of porous
N-doped carbon and single Ni atom active sites $(\mathrm{Ni}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ ). DFT calculations illustrated that PMS molecules tended to bond to Ni$\mathrm{N}_{4}$ with optimal bonding energy, leading to PMS activation to generate $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ and •OH (Figure 4a). Importantly, $\operatorname{Ni-SAs}@\operatorname{CN}$ was highly reusable and durable, while the leached Ni ions were almost undetectable (Figure 4b). In addition, the same mechanisms are also applicable to bimetallic MOF-derived SACs such as FeBi-NC.[88] Different from Ni-SAs@CN, the isolated Fe and Bi atoms were anchored by four N atoms to form Fe- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ and Bi- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ . FeBi-NC was more effective in activating PMS for RhB degradation than Fe-NC and Bi-NC due to dual active sites. DFT calculations unveiled that the dual sites processed stronger $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and longer $l_{0-0}$ determining high PMS activation ability compared with a single active center (Figure 4c). Besides, FeBi-NC also showed the excellent reusability of SACs, which can keep five cycles with no noticeable performance decline.
However, it is noted that many challenges still exist in exploring the radical pathways. For instance, it is uncertain whether the $\equiv\mathbf{M}^{(n+1)+}$ can be reduced back to $\equiv\mathrm{Mn^{+}}$ by intermediates or PMS/PDS after generating $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ and •OH, which largely determines the sustained catalytic performance of MOF-derived SACs.[91]
High-Valent Metal Species: The high spin state position with a large magnetic moment facilitates the coupling of PMS. It will greatly improve the oxidation potential and accelerate the electron transfer to form high-valent metal species $(\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}\mathbf{\Lambda}^{+}=\mathbf{O})$ . The main $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)+}{=}0$ are Fe(IV), $\mathrm{{Mn}(V)}$ , and ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{IV})$ which were generated from $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Equations (12)), ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ (Equations (13)), and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ (Equations (14) and (15)), respectively.[95] Besides, $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)+}{=}0$ has high selectivity to react with organic pollutants as well as can resist interference from coexisting substrates such as carbonates.[96]
$$
