In some cases, PDS with high redox potential may also react with active sites to generate ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . Du et al.[79] synthesized singleatom iron anchored on nitrogen-doped carbon (SAFe-N-C) to active PDS for chloramphenicol (CAP) degradation. The peroxide bond in PDS could be polarized because of the polar structure of Fe-N. Meanwhile, the positively charged C which resulted from N atoms in Fe- $\mathbf{\cdotN}_{x}$ would promote the generation of ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ (Equations (10) and (11)). It should be pointed out that the reaction kinetics of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(k=\approx10^{2}{-}10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ is much slower than radicals $(k=\approx10^{6}-10^{11}~\mathrm{{M}^{-1}~\mathrm{{s}^{-1})}}$ despite ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ having good selectivity for degrading target pollutants.[3,94] Therefore, most MOF-derived SACs achieved high removal efficiency by synergistic effects between radicals and ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .[45,78,81,89]
