Li et al.[44] synthesized the atomically dispersed Fe-N-C structure with $\mathrm{Fe}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{x}$ by direct pyrolysis. The Fe-N-C catalysts exhibited excellent catalytic activity for 2,2-bis (4-hydroxyphenyl) propane (BPA) degradation ( $100\%$ in $30~\mathrm{min}$ ), exceeding N-C by four times (Figure 4f). They found that Fe (III) in the Fe$\mathrm{N}_{x}$ sites would coordinate with the O atom in PMS to form intermediate complexes and produce $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{IV}}=}\mathrm{O}$ by heterolytic cleavage of $_{0-0}$ . The generated $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{IV}}=}\mathrm{O}$ could be transformed into Fe (III) again due to electrons transfer, which composed a circle to degrade organic pollutants (Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{g}\mid$ . Besides, Fe-N-C also showed excellent stability with no noticeable structure breaking after being used for three consecutive cycles as TEM images observed.
However, there are still three different aspects regarding the mechanisms of $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}{\mathrel{+}}=\mathbf{O}$ due to the lack of systematic mechanistic insights. I) Identification of the actual oxidation states of the metal in the reaction. Most studies have examined the oxidation states of the metal after the reaction by using ex situ XANES, but this is based on the unsubstantiated assumption that $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}{\mathrel{+}}=\mathbf{O}$ can be stably retained on the catalyst surface.[3,97] However, $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}{\mathrel{+}}=\mathbf{O}$ may be reduced back to low-valent species due to the reaction with PS or self-decay.[98] Thus, it is necessary to use other characterization techniques such as in situ XANES to identify accurately.[99] II) Ambiguous oxidation reaction cycle. Based on the above discussion, a single metal in $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{x}$ could be coordinated with an O atom in PMS to form $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}{\mathrel{+}}_{=0}$ , while it was uncertain how $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)}{\mathrel{+}}{=}0$ was transformed into low-valent species to complete the redox cycle such as self-decay or reduced by PS. And III) the relationship between $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)+}{=}0$ and the electronic configuration of MOF-derived SACs. It was suggested that the electronic configuration of the metal sites in the 3d orbital largely influenced their spin states by valence-bond theory and crystal theory.[100] Metals with unpaired electrons easily formed low-spin composites that tend to coordinate with electron transfer for degradation.[43] In this regard, it is essential to further confirm the above relationship between $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)+}{=}0$ and electronic configuration which can be reasonably controlled to solve practical problems.
## 3.1.2. Mediated ETP
Different from the ROS, electrons can also be directly transferred from the pollutants to the SACs and further decompose the PMS. Current studies reported two types of mechanisms about ETP in SR-AOPs: I) The contaminant first transfers electrons to the catalyst, which subsequently transfers them to the PMS/PDS; and II) PMS/PDS first forms an active compound with the catalyst via electron transfer, and then the catalyst oxidizes and reduces the contaminant.[101] Owing to the good conductivity of MOFs, MOFderived SACs are more likely to cause ETP rather than metal catalysts.[3b]
Similar to the literature on ROS, few studies have been proposed on the ETP of MOF-derived SACs. Owing to the low selectivity and instability of radicals, current studies have investigated reasonable methods to induce the conversion of free radicals to ETP. Among them, heteroatom doping (mainly N or S) is a favorable method in carbon materials. Therefore, it is viable to modify the MOF precursors to achieve the reconciliation of ROS and ETP for SACs. Furthermore, it can also enhance the correlation between PS activation efficiency and the molecular structures of MOF-derived SACs.
Generally speaking, MOF-derived SACs contain similar active sites but have different degradation pathways. Up to now, it has been confirmed that the conversion of ROS can be modulated by replacing different metal species or regulating the size of metal species.[102] I) Heteroatom doping. It can alter the coordination environment of single atoms and thus generate ROS selectively.[103] More specifically, heteroatom doping can achieve electronic structure modulation of active metal sites. Song et al.[104] established an asymmetrical configuration by boron doping, which provided a strong local electric field, thus further enabling O─O bond cleavage and efficient electron transfer. II) Surface vacancy defects engineering. Vacancy defects, as an inherent defect, have been proven to change the physicochemical properties of catalysts and generate unsaturated coordination sites. Meanwhile, the adsorption energy and charge transfer of PMS/PDS are optimized due to the generation of nucleophilic and electrophilic atomic centers on the catalyst surface.[105] III) Functional group modification. Functional group modifications can capture dispersed atomic sites such as amino.[106] For example, the amino-modified cobalt phthalocyanine anchored on graphene $\mathrm{(CoPc/G–NH_{2}};$ ) showed superior catalytic performance in the PMS-based reaction reported by Huang et al.[106] DFT calculation demonstrated that the modification of the ${\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ group narrowed the gap between the d-band center and the Fermi level of Co SAs. It would strengthen the charge transfer rate at the reaction interface and reduce the free energy barrier for the activation of PMS. IV) Selection of pollutants. The redox potentials of pollutants are essential to the ETP oxidation in the SAC/PMS system. Yin et al.[107] selected eight pollutants and determined their redox potentials by analyzing the half-wave potential $\displaystyle\left(\varphi_{1/2}\right)$ values. In the designed Mn-SAC/PMS system, overwhelming ETP oxidation for pollutants with low $\varphi_{1/2}$ values, ETP-dominated oxidation with noticeable radical oxidation for pollutants with moderate $\varphi_{1/2}$ values, and radicals-dominated oxidation for pollutants with high $\varphi_{1/2}$ values. Additionally, they also confirmed the positive correlation between the redox potential of pollutants and catalytic performances.
However, it remains a challenge to organize the relatively systematic relationship between active sites and degradation pathways.[108] This uncertainty may be related to the type of target pollutants, due to the distinct reaction constants between various pollutants and the common ROS.[109] Besides, the inaccurate judgment of ROS may be another reason for this uncertainty, because the transformation of sulfoxides to sulfones can be realized by addition to the heteroatoms of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or O-atom transfer of $\mathbf{M}^{(n+2)+}{=}0$ .[110] Therefore, more experiments and precise characterization should be required to solve this challenge.
## 3.2. Role of Adjacent Sites
The above discussion has proved that the $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{x}$ sites were the predominant active sites for the activation of PS. Unlike the active sites, the adjacent sites, which mainly include pyrrolic N and some metals, are generally applied to reduce the distance between the ROS generated by PMS activation and the target pollutants.[42,111] At the same time, the adjacent sites can optimize the distribution of bonding orbitals. This section will provide details on the identification of adjacent sites and the impact on the performance of MOF-derived SACs.
Previous studies have reported that nitrogen doping was an effective strategy to facilitate electronic transfer and improve the catalytic performance of MOF-derived SACs.[65] Thus, exploring the types and contents of N species that act as active site neighbors has attracted the researcher’s attention. Currently, highresolution XPS N 1s spectra are widely collected to quantify the types and contents of N dopants.[64] Li and co-workers[42] investigated the types and contents of N species in FeCo-NC by XPS N 1s spectra. As shown in Figure 5b, three peaks with binding energies at 398.4, 399.8, and $401.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ were deconvoluted, which could correspond to pyridinic, pyrrolic, and graphitic N, respectively. Particularly, the content of pyrrolic N was associated with pollutant adsorption efficiency. Furthermore, Yang and coworkers[62] found pollutant adsorption efficiency decreased after the Fenton-like reaction, along with an obvious decrease in the pyrrolic N content (Figure 5c). Surprisingly, simple annealing treatment could recover pollutant adsorption efficiency and the content of pyrrolic N, which implied pyrrolic N acted as a pollutant adsorption site (Figure 5d). Moreover, high-performance liquid chromatography and DFT calculations verified pyrrolic N as the electron donor and -OH of pollutant as the electron acceptor (Figure 5e). Generally speaking, the existence of pyrrolic N highly reduced the distance between pollutants and ROS generated at active sites, which realized efficient target pollutant degradation by MOF-derived SACs.
In addition to pyrrolic N, metal sites such as Cu can also affect performance. DFT calculations revealed that the adjacent Cu atoms in Fe/Cu-N-C effectively optimized the distribution of bonding orbitals among the Fe 3d orbitals,[89] then it subsequently promoted the adsorption and cracking of PDS and the oxidative decomposition of pollutants by active sites (Figure 5f). Furthermore, the electron supplemented by adjacent Cu along the Fe─Cu bonds can maintain the low valence state of Fe atoms to effectively activate PDS (Figure 5g). Due tothe synergistic effects of Fe and Cu sites, the Fe/Cu-N-C significantly enhanced the CAP removal rate from 0.073 to $0.093\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . Interestingly, the dual-site adsorption and activation mode will also be established because two adjacent metal sites can change the adsorption pattern of PS.[112] Compared with other ROS generation, this mode can avert the breakup of $_{0-0}$ bond and enhance the electron rearrangement, which ultimately promotes efficient and highselective ETP to enhance organics degradation. Wang et al.[112] fabricated am Fe-Co dual-atom catalyst (FeCo-DAC) with FeCo$\mathrm{N}_{6}$ moiety anchored in the carbon skeleton. The reaction kinetic of BPA was significantly boosted by regulating PMS adsorption mode. Based on DFT calculations, PMS adsorption mode from single-site adsorption (adsorption of oxygen atoms from peroxide bonds in PMS) to dual-site adsorption (adsorption of two-ended oxygen atoms double-bonded to S in PMS) was precisely regulated to achieve nearly $100\%$ ETP.
For various pollutant types, the patterns of ROS in reacting target pollutants exhibit much difference. In the radical system, $\mathrm{SO_{4}^{\bullet-}}$ and $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ degrade organic pollutants primarily by similar mechanisms, including single electron transfer, hydrogen atom abstraction (HAA), and radical addition formation.[113,116] In the nonradical system, it is elaborated to decipher the reaction pathways and the only nonradical species that is relatively well understood might be ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ .[113] Electrophilic addition is the typical reaction pathway for ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ to react with unsaturated compounds via acting on double bonds or phenols.[117] For high-valent metal species, there are three possible pathways containing electron transfer, HAA, and oxygen atom transfer (OAT).[118] Thanks to the presence of OAT, high-valent metals can react with sulfoxides (e.g., methyl phenyl sulfoxide (PMSO) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)) and produce the corresponding sulfones (e.g., ${\mathrm{PMSO}}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{DMSO}}_{2}$ ) which can be applied as chemical probes to identify pathways initiated by high-valent metal species.[119] In particular, PMS may degrade pollutants, especially those with electrondonating groups like tetrabromobisphenol A (TBBPA), by direct oxidation rather than activating.[120] In conclusion, the role of different sites of the catalyst, how the active species attack the pollutant, and the assessment of the performance through kinetics and thermodynamics are three essential parts of the mechanism to be explored. Meanwhile, a fundamental platform should be established as soon as possible to mediate appropriate pathways to degrade target pollutants.
## 4. Characterization Techniques for Mechanism Studies
Identifying different ROS and determining active sites are very crucial for optimizing the process.[62b,121] Besides, theoretical calculations are also widely applied to determine active sites. In this section, the characterization techniques for exploring the mechanisms will be summarized systematically, which in turn contribute to precisely analyzing the degradation process.
## 4.1. Identification of ROS
Radical quenching experiments are used to scavenge one (or several) ROS through specific chemical reagents to determine their functions in oxidation.[122] Based on the distinct reaction rates at which ROS react with the quenching reagent and organics, this process is competitive.[87] To assess the effectiveness of the chosen scavenger, a competition kinetic rate coefficient $(\beta)$ is used. The scavenger nearly eliminates the target ROS when the theoretical f value is less than 0.01.[122] Importantly, the contributions of ETP are not directly evaluated by this method unless the exclusion methods are used because no dedicated scavenger is available. Table 2 shows common quenching agents corresponding to different ROS.
However, it should be noted that there are some debatable aspects in the applications made by some scavengers in PS-based nonradical systems. First, the scavenger (e.g., furfuryl alcohol (FFA)) may react directly with the PS when it is in high concentration, leading to inhibiting oxidation efficiency.[124] Second, most scavengers of ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ and high-valent metal species (e.g., FFA and PMSO) can react with •OH and $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ . It will result in exaggerated roles of nonradical pathways.[110] Third, scavengers of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can consume PMS and take part in ETP as electron donors, resulting in a greater inflated function of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the nonradical systems.[122] Finally, some scavengers can also alter solution properties, such as solution $\mathsf{p H}$ or polarity, eventually leading to the inhibition of PS adsorption/activation and adsorption/oxidation of organic pollutants.[122] Therefore, certain additional strategies are devised to further understand the function of various ROS.[125] For instance, it is possible to infer the concentration of a particular ROS indirectly by solving a kinetic equation with the four processes: model establishment, kinetics experiment, data computation, and result verification/prediction.[126]
Apart from radical quenching experiments, electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) has also been applied to detect ROS (free radicals, ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and others) by using particular trapping reagents.[96] EPR is used to determine ROS by converting the adducts formed by the reaction of the ROS with the corresponding spin agents into a visual signal map. Currently, DMPO (5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline $N\mathrm{.}$ -oxide) and TEMP (2,2,6,6- tetramethylpiperidine) are the most widely applied spin agents for ROS.[127] Xu and co-workers[83] performed EPR measurements to observe radical production through the FeSA-CN catalyst. When FeSA-CN was incorporated into the system, EPR spectra identified two adduct signals representing DMPO-HO• and DMPO- $.\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ (Figure 6a). Meanwhile, a triplet EPR spectrum of TEMP suggesting ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ was observed (Figure 6b). All the phenomena demonstrated that •OH, $\mathrm{SO_{4}^{\bullet-}}$ , and ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were generated during the activation of PMS by FeSA-CN/PMS.
It is worth pointing out that the signals of EPR are sometimes misleading in PS-based systems. The presence of TEMPO does not always imply the presence of ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , because ETP can also potentially trigger the formation of ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . Besides, some studies speculated that the strong EPR signal of ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ may be an illusion caused by the interaction between PMS and TEMP.[85,128] In some cases, DMPO might be oxidized by one electron to generate DMPO•OH without the involvement of •OH.[129] As a result, it is challenging to ascertain the contributions of each ROS in the PS system, because EPR signals only represent ROS at a semiquantitative or qualitative level.[122] For instance, although there were substantial signals of $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ in the EPR, ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was the major ROS during the oxidation of BPA.[109] It is hypothesized that the dominant functioning species of ROS in the multiple ROS system may be impacted by the structure of contaminants.[109]
Recently, chemical probes have been applied to further evaluate the contributions of ROS and model the kinetic of contaminant elimination in realistic water treatment.[130] In general, common probes include para-Chlorobenzoic acid (p-CBA),[131] chloroform $\mathsf{\Gamma}(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3})$ ,[131] and tetrachloromethane $\mathrm{(CCl_{4})}$ ,[130] which should be spiked in low concentration to avoid causing considerable changes to reaction mechanisms.[132] Zhou and coworkers[133] established a degradation kinetic model with probe compounds to systematically evaluate the ROS concentrations during PMS activation. The calculations represented the contribution of ROS, PMS, UV (reaction condition), and adsorption of waste-tire carbon to the degradation of a compound following degradation profiles under specific conditions (Figure 6c). Notably, ${}^{1}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ was the predominant ROS in the reaction system, with $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{\bullet-}$ and $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ contributing more to the decomposition of most of the probe chemicals, despite the relatively low content of total radicals (Figure 6d,e). In conclusion, compared with radical quenching experiments, the roles of ROS in micropollutant abatement based on the PS-based process can be realistically revealed by the probe-based kinetic model.[134]
## 4.2. Identification of ETP
The three methods mentioned above are not convenient to identify the ETP. To solve this problem, recent studies have reported a series of advanced characterization/analytical techniques to identify the ETP directly in SR-AOPs, such as electrochemical analysis, product analysis, quantitative structure– activity relationships (QSARs), in situ Fourier transform, and Raman spectrum.[43,82,135] Comparatively speaking, Raman spectra and electrochemical analysis are used frequently in MOFderived SACs, which will be discussed in the following part.
In addition to in situ analysis techniques, electrochemical analysis, which is applied to measure the redox reaction at the solid/liquid interfaces of the electrode, can provide direct evidence to validate the ETP in SR-AOPs.[122] Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), as one of the electrochemical analyses, could be applied to investigate ETP in PS systems by monitoring the current density. Yang et al.[82] found that the interaction between BPA and metastable reactive complexes led to an apparent increase in the current density in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}20+\mathrm{PMS}$ system. However, LSV is unable to monitor the direction of current flow, and excess organics will obstruct the current signals by influencing the charge transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ or open circuit potential.[122] To solve the problem of the direction of current, chronoamperometry (CP) was used to identify the direction of electron transfer through a negative or a positive current signal in the presence of organics and activated PS.[13,137]
Another prevalent electrochemical analysis is electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), which is applied to estimate the electron-transfer capacity of the catalysts. EIS can reflect the electron-transfer resistance through the semicircle in the high-frequency range of the Nyquist diagram. The smaller the diameter of the semicircle is, the smaller the electrontransfer resistance of the catalysts.[138] As shown in Figure 6g, the greatly reduced electron-transfer resistance of the FeSA$\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}20{+}\mathrm{PMS}{+}\mathrm{BPA}$ system was demonstrated by a smaller semicircle diameter.[82] To sum up, electrochemical analysis offers great convenience in studying the mechanisms of ETP by converting abstract electron transfer into concrete electrical signals.
## 4.3. Theoretical Calculations
As mentioned above, conventional characterization techniques can reveal information about ROS and ETP. However, direct observation of the interaction pathways of PS with SACs is still challenging, leading to less mechanistic insights for their generation. DFT calculations, as one of the theoretical calculations, are used to solve this defect.[139] In general, DFT calculations aim to determine active sites and predict reaction intermediates at the molecular level, mainly including molecular decay, molecular alignment, and electron transfer.[3b] This repository of information can help guide the design of more efficient, robust, and durable SACs with high atomic activity and selectivity in future PS-based water treatment.
Typically, four parameters of DFT calculations are calculated to determine the active sites used to activate PS in MOFderived SACs. They are adsorption energy $(E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ , $_{0-0}$ bond length of PS $(l_{\mathrm{{O-O}}})$ , the reaction barrier $(\Delta G)$ , and charge density difference. $\left[24\mathrm{c},139\mathrm{a},140\right]$ Favorable catalytic sites lead to more negative $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and longer $l_{0-0}$ to enhance PS adsorption and activation.[135a,141] Calculating the $\Delta G$ of the transition states during PS activation can also be useful in the investigation of possible reaction pathways.[135a] Remarkably, $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ between the PMS molecules and the catalysts are more necessary to obtain, which contributes to identifying the active sites. According to the Sabatier principle, the binding $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ between PMS and catalysts should be strong enough to facilitate PMS activation and subsequent activation, while it also should be weak enough to prevent reaction intermediates from poisoning active sites.[142] In other words, the binding $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ of the active site should maintain an intermediate state to balance strength and weakness. Yang et al.[62b] carried out DFT calculations to provide further insights into the $\mathrm{Mn}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ sites of the Mn-ISAs $@{\mathsf{C N}}$ . They analyzed the completely relaxed atomic configurations of the PMS on the $\mathrm{Mn\mathrm{-}N_{4}}$ sites, the MnO (100) surface, and graphitic $\mathrm{~N~}$ sites while calculating the corresponding $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ to be 2.73, 2.59, and $2.11\mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure 6h). The results showed that the $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ of the PMS on the $\mathrm{Mn\mathrm{-}N_{4}}$ site had the most intermediate strength which was regarded as the optimal active site for activating PMS based on the Sabatier principle. Generally speaking, current studies found that this balance was mainly achieved in the $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ configurations according to the Sabatier principle.
The difference in charge density is a direct reflection of the charge distribution of different structures and the interactions with other components, which can contribute to understanding the relationship between PS and catalysts.[24] According to the charge density difference, the electron flow between PMS and catalysts can be observed visually. In other words, the central metal atoms in the M-N-C commonly show the highest positive potential and the lowest electron density which might attract the PMS served as an active site. DFT can also further calculate the highest occupied molecular orbital of M-N-C and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital of PMS according to the frontier orbital theory.[63] It demonstrated that M-N-C tended to donate electrons while PMS could capture them. In this regard, the Mulliken charges and spin population can attest to the existence and types of ROS that attack the surface-adsorbed organics. Ren et al.[63] found ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ frequently displayed spin population and approximately zero charges.
## 5. Conclusion and Perspectives
The rational design of efficient catalysts is of utmost significance for SR-AOPs which have been regarded as one of the bright stars in AOPs owing to their high oxidation potential and less $\mathsf{p H}$ dependence. It is interesting to note that the emergence of SACs has eliminated the disparity between heterogenous catalysts and homogenous catalysts and exhibited tremendous potential in SR-AOPs. In particular, MOF-derived SACs are becoming the prospective candidates for SR-AOPs because of their maximum atomic efficiency while inheriting the distinct stability and flexibility of MOFs. In this review, we first particularly highlight the advantages of MOFs as derived precursors, while the synthesis strategies of MOF-derived SACs are also described in detail. Specially, we provide insights into the reaction mechanisms according to the recent progress in these catalysts for SR-AOPs, especially the roles of different reaction sites. Besides, relevant characterization techniques are in-depth discussed which can provide the possibility of a thorough comprehension of mechanismperformance correlations. The systematic analysis highlights that MOF-derived SACs can offer significantly higher reaction efficiency and lower metal consumption.
Despite such advantages of MOF-derived SACs in SR-AOPs, the research on these MOF-derived SACs is still in its early stages. Enormous challenges and prospects should be taken into consideration comprehensively which are attributed to achieving higher performance of MOF-derived SACs and promoting the practical applications in SR-AOPs.
1) The types of precursors and target metals derived from MOFs should be expanded. Currently, only a small proportion of MOFs, particularly ZIFs, are used for derivation into SACs. Expanding precursor types facilitates the preparation of different morphologies of MOF-derived SACs, which provides more options for solving diverse environmental problems. In addition, the kinds of target metals are also commonly applied for SACs and they play a decisive role in reacting with PS.
2) Reasonable regulation of the coordination arrangements of single atoms. The performance of catalysts depends on the efficient control of the coordination structures, especially the number of allocations. In general, the unsaturated coordination environments lead to a redistribution of the spins of the centers, which alters the adsorption of the intermediates. Therefore, it is a practical way to design unsaturated coordination of the asymmetric active center to further enhance the catalytic capacity.
3) Development of efficient and economical characterization techniques. Presently, it is still ambiguous about the association between catalytic reaction and intermediate states of MOF-derived SACs. And the characterization techniques are relatively expensive. Therefore, exploring advanced and lowcost characterization instruments is imperative to comprehensively investigate the link between microstructure and reaction mechanisms.
4) Introduction of emerging predictive models. DFT is widely used as a powerful tool to analyze interaction pathways. However, owing to the constraints of time and computational resources, DFT is not appropriate for filtering a lot of models and predictions. Machine learning (ML) and QSARs are convenient and reliable tools for building predictive models of material properties. The combination of the above tools would contribute to learning the evolution of ROS from the molecular to the macroscopic level.
5) Increasing metal loading. The metal loading in MOF-derived SACs is an important parameter for determining the turnover frequencies $(\mathrm{min^{-1}})$ and kinetics of SACs in SR-AOPs. However, previous studies have confirmed that the metal loadings present in SACs are no more than $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ owing to the high surface energy of single atoms. Therefore, it is crucial to investigate effective strategies such as heteroatom doping to increase the metal loading of MOF-derived SACs without severe agglomeration.
6) Further investigation of the mechanisms. Despite the revealed mechanisms of SR-AOPs are gradually becoming systematic, there still exist uncertainties. For example, the inherent active sites of MOF-derived SACs cannot be accurately recognized, especially their synergistic effects in SR-AOPs. Besides, it is doubtful to point out the origins of several oxidation pathways based on geometry–activity relationships. Finally, it is also challenged to fully investigate the pathway that highvalent species formed in SR-AOPs, while the methods of regulating radical/nonfree radical pathways.
7) Maximizing the potential performance of MOF-derived SACs. Most degradation experiments illustrated that MOF-derived SACs completely decomposed selective pollutants in a short time. However, it is tough to assess how far the existing material designs have achieved their fullest extent for SR-AOPs. Therefore, it is valuable to find out strategies to further unleash the catalytic potential. For example, similar to applications in spinel ferrites $\mathrm{(MFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ or bimetallic nanoclusters, it is reasonable to explore SACs with more than one metal to further enhance degradation efficiency by less time and catalysts, and broaden the fields of application.
8) Considerations for practical applications. It is underconsidered to simply put MOF-derived SACs into water bodies, no matter which substrates as precursors. Due to the significant consumption involved in separating catalysts from liquids, it is a reasonable alternative to immobilize SACs on membranes or fixed-beds reactors. Moreover, many disturbing factors are usually presented in real water, such as inorganic ions and microorganisms. They may suppress the catalytic efficiency of MOF-derived SACs through redox reactions or competition with the active sites.
